  <article>
    <h1>In praise of the maintainer</h1>
    <p class="byline">By Priya Raghunathan · 4 November 2025</p>

    <p>Every conference talk I have ever sat through celebrates the person
    who built the thing. Nobody gives a keynote about the person who kept
    it running. I want to argue that this is exactly backwards, and I want
    to argue it from the only evidence I have: the four years, from 2019
    to 2023, that I spent maintaining the shift-scheduling system at
    Bracken &amp; Doyle, a warehouse logistics firm.</p>

    <p>The system was not glamorous. It assigned shifts to about 9,000
    warehouse workers across 23 sites, and in a normal week it processed
    roughly 30,000 shift assignments. It was written before I arrived and
    it will outlive everyone who currently touches it. My team — there
    were three of us — did not build it. We kept it honest.</p>

    <p>In 2021 the company approved a full rewrite, budgeted at eighteen
    months. The rewrite was cancelled after fourteen months, with roughly
    $2 million spent and nothing shipped to production. I do not tell this
    story to gloat. The rewrite team was talented, and the plan was not
    absurd. It failed for the boring reason most rewrites fail: the old
    system's real specification lived in a decade of accumulated fixes
    that no document recorded.</p>

    <p>Here is the part that never makes it into the keynote. Over the
    same fourteen months, our three-person maintenance team shipped 41
    releases, and the production incident count fell from 31 in the
    quarter the rewrite started to 6 in the quarter it was cancelled. The
    unglamorous work was not merely cheaper. It was the only work that
    was actually improving the thing the business ran on.</p>

    <p>The lesson I draw is that stewardship compounds and novelty mostly
    does not. A maintainer who closes one confusing failure mode makes
    every future incident easier to diagnose; a greenfield team learns
    things the production system already knew. I think the industry's
    rewards — promotion criteria, conference programs, the word
    "impact" itself — are calibrated to the wrong one of these two
    curves.</p>

    <p>I left Bracken &amp; Doyle in 2023, and the scheduling system is
    still running. I consider that the proudest line on my résumé, and I
    have stopped apologizing for it.</p>
  </article>

